    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Sterling & Maritime Holdings [2023] UKSC 42, the Supreme Court elucidated the duty of care in maritime operations, anchoring it on the 'foreseeability of harm' within the maritime corridor's context. The court navigated the delicate balance between the proximity principle from Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562 and the broader negligence framework from Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] 2 AC 605. It further anchored its decision in the statutory confines of the Merchant Shipping Act 1995, which necessitates a more circumscribed duty of care. The judgment also referenced the House of Lords' decision in The Wagon Mound (No. 1) [1961] AC 388, which established foreseeability as a cornerstone of duty of care, and the House of Lords' ruling in The Wagon Mound (No. 2) [1967] AC 388, which refined the scope of liability for indirect consequences. The court's nuanced approach ensures that the duty of care remains contextually tethered to the specificities of maritime operations, providing a clear precedent for future cases involving maritime liability.",
      "source": "target",
